---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
---
<Layout title="Support – VoiceFlow" description="Detailed VoiceFlow setup instructions for Android keyboards, the floating bubble, and voice-input apps.">
  <section class="page-header"><div class="container"><p class="eyebrow">Support</p><h1>Use VoiceFlow with your keyboard.</h1><p class="lede">VoiceFlow works alongside your current keyboard. Choose the setup that matches how you want to dictate.</p></div></section>

  <section class="section"><div class="container narrow prose">
    <h2>Before you start</h2>
    <ol>
      <li>Complete the VoiceFlow setup wizard.</li>
      <li>Allow microphone access.</li>
      <li>Choose and download a local voice model, or configure your cloud provider.</li>
      <li>If you use the bubble, enable <strong>VoiceFlow</strong> under Android <strong>Accessibility</strong> settings.</li>
      <li>Test the setup in a normal text field, such as a messaging or notes app.</li>
    </ol>
    <p>VoiceFlow can work completely on-device when you use a local engine. Cloud transcription and cloud cleanup use the provider and API key configured on your device.</p>

    <h2>SwiftKey</h2>
    <article class="support-card support-card-wide">
      <h3>Use the SwiftKey microphone button</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Open an app with a text field and tap the field.</li>
        <li>Tap the microphone button on the SwiftKey toolbar.</li>
        <li>SwiftKey opens Android’s voice-input panel, where you can choose VoiceFlow if it is available.</li>
        <li>Tap the VoiceFlow microphone, speak, then tap it again to stop.</li>
        <li>VoiceFlow returns the transcript to SwiftKey and the focused field.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>This is the most direct keyboard-microphone integration because SwiftKey invokes Android’s standard speech-recognition interface. VoiceFlow may receive the language or prompt requested by the calling app.</p>
    </article>

    <h2>Gboard</h2>
    <article class="support-card support-card-wide">
      <h3>Use the VoiceFlow bubble or VoiceFlow Keyboard</h3>
      <p>Gboard normally keeps its microphone button connected to Google voice typing. It does not generally hand that button to another speech recognizer. Use one of these two alternatives:</p>
      <ol>
        <li><strong>Floating bubble:</strong> leave Gboard selected, focus a text field, and tap the VoiceFlow bubble above the app. Speak, stop, and VoiceFlow inserts the text into the field.</li>
        <li><strong>VoiceFlow Keyboard:</strong> enable VoiceFlow in Android keyboard settings, switch to it with the keyboard/globe button, and use its microphone control. After committing text, VoiceFlow returns to the previous keyboard when Android allows it.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>You do not need to replace Gboard permanently. VoiceFlow is registered as an auxiliary voice keyboard and can be switched to only when needed.</p>
    </article>

    <h2>Samsung Keyboard</h2>
    <article class="support-card support-card-wide">
      <h3>Use the VoiceFlow bubble or VoiceFlow Keyboard</h3>
      <p>Samsung Keyboard generally keeps its own microphone action connected to Samsung voice input. For VoiceFlow, use the same two paths as with Gboard:</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Keep Samsung Keyboard active and tap the VoiceFlow floating bubble, or</li>
        <li>Enable and select VoiceFlow Keyboard from Android’s keyboard switcher.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>The bubble is convenient when you want to keep Samsung Keyboard visible. The VoiceFlow Keyboard is useful when you want live dictated text to appear as composing text in the focused field.</p>
    </article>

    <h2>VoiceFlow Keyboard</h2>
    <article class="support-card support-card-wide">
      <h3>Enable it once, then switch to it when needed</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Open VoiceFlow and tap <strong>Voice typing keyboard</strong>, or open Android keyboard settings manually.</li>
        <li>Enable <strong>VoiceFlow voice typing</strong>.</li>
        <li>Focus a text field in any app.</li>
        <li>Open the Android keyboard switcher using the keyboard or globe button and select VoiceFlow.</li>
        <li>Tap the microphone to start and tap again to stop.</li>
        <li>VoiceFlow commits the final transcript into the focused field, then normally switches back to your previous keyboard.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>VoiceFlow Keyboard is an auxiliary voice keyboard, not a replacement for your regular keyboard. It is designed to be selected temporarily. With the system speech engine, partial words can appear while you speak; other engines insert the completed result when recording stops.</p>
      <p>If the keyboard switcher does not show VoiceFlow, confirm that it is enabled in Android settings and restart the target app after enabling it.</p>
    </article>

    <h2>Voice-input popup and websites</h2>
    <article class="support-card support-card-wide">
      <h3>Use VoiceFlow when an app asks Android for voice input</h3>
      <p>Some apps and websites open Android’s standard speech-recognition interface instead of using a keyboard-specific voice feature. When that happens, Android can show the VoiceFlow voice-input popup.</p>
      <ol>
        <li>Tap the app or website’s voice-search or microphone control.</li>
        <li>Choose VoiceFlow if Android asks which voice-input service to use.</li>
        <li>Record and stop in the VoiceFlow popup.</li>
        <li>VoiceFlow returns one final result to the requesting app.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>This flow is controlled by the calling app. If it does not use Android’s standard speech-recognition request, use the bubble or VoiceFlow Keyboard instead.</p>
    </article>

    <h2>Floating bubble</h2>
    <article class="support-card support-card-wide">
      <h3>Works independently of your keyboard</h3>
      <ol>
        <li>Enable the VoiceFlow Accessibility Service when Android asks.</li>
        <li>Leave Gboard, Samsung Keyboard, SwiftKey, or another keyboard selected.</li>
        <li>Focus a normal text field and tap the floating VoiceFlow button.</li>
        <li>Speak, then tap stop. VoiceFlow inserts the result into the focused field.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>Some apps use custom editors or deliberately block accessibility text insertion. In that case VoiceFlow copies the transcript to the clipboard so you can paste it manually.</p>
    </article>

    <h2>When something does not work</h2>
    <div class="support-grid">
      <article class="support-card"><h3>No microphone or no words</h3><p>Check Android microphone permission, close another app that may be using the microphone, and retry from VoiceFlow’s test screen.</p></article>
      <article class="support-card"><h3>The bubble does nothing</h3><p>Confirm the Accessibility Service is enabled and that a standard text field is focused. Some secure or custom fields reject insertion.</p></article>
      <article class="support-card"><h3>No local transcription</h3><p>Open the Models tab and verify that a model for the selected engine is installed. Large Gemma models may take longer to load.</p></article>
      <article class="support-card"><h3>Cloud transcription fails</h3><p>Check the configured API key, provider endpoint, network connection, account limits, and the provider’s request format.</p></article>
    </div>

    <p class="support-note">For privacy and legal information, read the <a href={`${base}privacy-policy`}>Privacy Policy</a> and <a href={`${base}terms`}>Terms &amp; Conditions</a>. Do not include API keys, private recordings, or private dictated text in any support request.</p>
  </div></section>
</Layout>
